Q: .NET Core API Swagger always returning 401 A: <p>Found it: <code>Name = &quot;Auth&quot;</code> in your <code>AddSecurityDefinition</code>.</p>
<pre><code>options.AddSecurityDefinition(&quot;Bearer&quot;, new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    In = ParameterLocation.Header,
    Description = &quot;Please enter your Bearer Token&quot;,
    Name = &quot;Auth&quot;,   // &lt;-- bug: this makes Swagger send a header literally named &quot;Auth&quot;
    Type = SecuritySchemeType.ApiKey,
    Scheme = &quot;Bearer&quot;
});
</code></pre>
<p>With <code>Type = SecuritySchemeType.ApiKey</code>, the <code>Name</code> property is the actual HTTP header name Swagger UI will send. You set it to <code>&quot;Auth&quot;</code>, so Swagger is sending your token in a header called <code>Auth</code>, not <code>Authorization</code>. Your JWT middleware only reads the standard <code>Authorization</code> header — so it never sees a token at all, and you get 401 regardless of how correct the token itself is. Postman worked because you were (correctly) putting it in the <code>Authorization</code> header there.</p>
<p><strong>Fix:</strong></p>
<pre><code>options.AddSecurityDefinition(&quot;Bearer&quot;, new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    In = ParameterLocation.Header,
    Description = &quot;Please enter your Bearer Token&quot;,
    Name = &quot;Authorization&quot;,   // &lt;-- fixed
    Type = SecuritySchemeType.ApiKey,
    Scheme = &quot;Bearer&quot;
});
</code></pre>
<p>Small secondary cleanup: your <code>AddSecurityRequirement</code> sets <code>Scheme = &quot;oauth2&quot;</code> on the reference, which is inconsistent (should be <code>&quot;Bearer&quot;</code>) — it doesn't cause your 401 since resolution happens via the <code>Id</code> reference, but worth fixing for clarity:</p>
<pre><code>options.AddSecurityRequirement(new OpenApiSecurityRequirement {
{
    new OpenApiSecurityScheme 
    {
        Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = &quot;Bearer&quot; },
        Scheme = &quot;Bearer&quot;,   // fixed
        Name = &quot;Bearer&quot;,
        In = ParameterLocation.Header
    },
    new string[] {}
}});
</code></pre>

Q: JavaScript for a collapsible which expands on click A: <p>The issue isn't your script placement — it's .closest().</p>
<p>Element.closest() only searches the element itself and its ancestors (parents, going up the tree). It never looks at siblings or children. In your <strong>HTML</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;update&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;update-header&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;??? 2026&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p class=&quot;update-content&quot;&gt;placeholder&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><code>.update-header</code> and <code>.update-content</code> are siblings — both children of <code>.update</code>. Since <code>.update-content</code> is not an ancestor of <code>.update-header</code>, <code>this.closest(&quot;.update-content&quot;)</code> will always return null, and calling <code>.classList.toggle()</code> on null throws a TypeError in the console (silently breaking the rest of your click handler).</p>
<p><strong>Fix — go up to the shared parent, then find the content inside it:</strong></p>
<pre><code>let headers = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.update-header&quot;);

headers.forEach((header) =&gt; {
  header.addEventListener('click', function () {
    let updateContent = this.closest(&quot;.update&quot;).querySelector(&quot;.update-content&quot;);
    updateContent.classList.toggle('active');
  });
});
</code></pre>
<p>Or, since <code>.update-content</code> is always the very next sibling in your markup, this is simpler and slightly cheaper:</p>
<pre><code>let headers = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;.update-header&quot;);

headers.forEach((header) =&gt; {
  header.addEventListener('click', function () {
    this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('active');
  });
});
</code></pre>
<p>Either works — <code>closest(&quot;.update&quot;).querySelector(...)</code> is more resilient if you ever add more elements between header and content, <code>nextElementSibling</code> is simpler if the structure stays exactly as-is.</p>

Q: mkdocs - dynamic list of child pages A: <p>Following the inspiration from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/76889635/2530121">@fralau's answer</a>, this is my solution in my <code>main.py</code> in my documentation root:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code># main.py
&quot;&quot;&quot;Macros for use within Jinja templates in Markdown files.&quot;&quot;&quot;
from pathlib import Path

def define_env(env) -&gt; None:
    @env.macro
    def local_toc(depth: int = 1) -&gt; str:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;
        Local table of contents macro.

        Generate a table of contents of all Markdown pages at or below
        the current level of the directory tree.  Intended to be used
        in `index.md` pages as follows:

            {{ local_toc(depth=2) }}

        Args:
            depth:  How many levels deep to generate the table of
                contents.

        Returns:
            The Markdown list of sibling and child pages, complete with
            links.
        &quot;&quot;&quot;
        file_being_processed = Path(env.page.file.abs_src_path)
        return &quot;\n&quot;.join(
            _process_directory(
                file_being_processed.parent,
                current_depth=1,
                max_depth=depth,
            )
        )

def _process_directory(
    directory: Path,
    current_depth: int = 1,
    max_depth: int = 1,
) -&gt; list[str]:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Generate the local table of contents for a given directory.

    Note:
        Recurses into subdirectories if `max_depth` is not yet reached.

    Args:
        directory:  The directory to process.
        current_depth:  The current depth in the hierarchy being
            processed, relative to the root where processing started.
        max_depth:  The maximum desired depth of the complete table of
            contents.

    Returns:
            The Markdown list of sibling and child pages, complete with
            links, appropriately indented based on `current_depth`.
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    lines: list[str] = []
    if current_depth &gt; max_depth:
        return lines
    indent = &quot;    &quot; * (current_depth - 1)
    for item in sorted(directory.iterdir(), key=lambda _: _.name):
        if item.suffix == &quot;.md&quot; and item.name != &quot;index.md&quot;:
            title = _get_markdown_title(item) or item.name
            link = item.relative_to(item.parents[current_depth - 1])
            lines.append(indent + f&quot;- [{title}]({link})&quot;)
        elif item.is_dir():
            index_file = item / &quot;index.md&quot;
            if index_file.exists():
                title = _get_markdown_title(index_file) or item.name
                link = index_file.relative_to(item.parents[current_depth - 1])
                lines.append(indent + f&quot;- [{title}]({link})&quot;)
            elif list(item.glob(&quot;*.md&quot;)):
                lines.append(indent + f&quot;- {item.name}&quot;)
            lines.extend(
                _process_directory(item, current_depth + 1, max_depth)
            )
    return lines

def _get_markdown_title(file: Path) -&gt; str | None:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Get the title from a Markdown file.

    Args:
        file:  The file to read.

    Returns:
        The first top-level (`#`) heading content, or `None` if no
        top-level heading is present.
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    with open(file, &quot;r&quot;, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as f:
        for line in f:
            stripped = line.strip()
            if stripped.startswith(&quot;# &quot;):
                return stripped[2:].strip()
    return None

</code></pre>

Q: How can I see the command history across all PowerShell sessions in Windows Server 2016? A: <p>This is the solution that got me as close as pssible to a persistent history list with ! type functionality.</p>
<p>Test if you have a $PROFILE:</p>
<pre><code>Test-Path $PROFILE
</code></pre>
<p>If False, create $PROFILE:</p>
<pre><code>New-Item -ItemType File -Path $PROFILE -Force
</code></pre>
<p>Edit profile:</p>
<pre><code>notepad $PROFILE
</code></pre>
<p>Add the following to your $PROFILE in Notepad:</p>
<pre><code>Set-PSReadLineOption -HistorySaveStyle SaveIncrementally
Set-PSReadLineOption -MaximumHistoryCount 10000
Set-PSReadLineOption -HistoryNoDuplicates
Set-PSReadLineOption -HistorySearchCursorMovesToEnd

function Get-CleanHistory {
    $raw = Get-Content (Get-PSReadLineOption).HistorySavePath
    $merged = @()
    $buffer = $null
    foreach ($line in $raw) {
        $buffer = if ($null -ne $buffer) { &quot;$buffer`n$line&quot; } else { $line }
        if ($buffer.TrimEnd().EndsWith('`')) {
            $buffer = $buffer.TrimEnd().TrimEnd('`')   # strip continuation marker, keep buffering
        } else {
            $merged += $buffer
            $buffer = $null
        }
    }
    if ($buffer) { $merged += $buffer }
    $merged | Get-Unique
}

function rh {
    param([int]$Id)
    $lines = Get-CleanHistory
    if ($Id -lt 1 -or $Id -gt $lines.Count) {
        Write-Warning &quot;No such history entry: $Id&quot;
        return
    }
    $cmd = $lines[$Id - 1]
    Write-Host &quot;&gt; $cmd&quot; -ForegroundColor Cyan
    Invoke-Expression $cmd
}

function hist {
    Get-CleanHistory | ForEach-Object -Begin {$i=1} -Process { &quot;{0,4}  {1}&quot; -f $i,$_; $i++ }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Save and exit notepad.</p>
<p>Load edited $PROFILE:</p>
<pre><code>. $PROFILE
</code></pre>
<p>If that fails, update execution policy:</p>
<pre><code>Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser
</code></pre>
<p>Load edited $PROFILE:</p>
<pre><code>. $PROFILE
</code></pre>
<p>You now have hist (instead of history). You can run previous commands using: rh {number}.</p>

Q: Perform template parameter type checking and make a decision based on it A: <p>Use <code>if constexpr</code> (C++17) to branch at compile time on whether <code>T</code> and <code>U</code> match:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
class widget {
    template &lt;typename U&gt; friend class widget; // let widget&lt;U&gt; access widget&lt;T&gt;'s privates
private:
    T data;
public:
    widget() = default;
    widget(const T&amp; value) : data(value) {}

    template &lt;typename U&gt;
    widget(const widget&lt;U&gt;&amp; other) {
        if constexpr (std::is_same_v&lt;T, U&gt;) {
            data = other.data;
        } else {
            throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;Type mismatch in widget copy construction&quot;);
        }
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Since you already know at compile time whether <code>T == U</code>, most C++ style guides would push you toward rejecting the mismatched case as a compile error rather than a runtime exception, since the compiler already has all the information needed:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename U&gt;
widget(const widget&lt;U&gt;&amp; other) {
    static_assert(std::is_same_v&lt;T, U&gt;, &quot;widget&lt;T&gt; can only be copy-constructed from another widget&lt;T&gt;&quot;);
    data = other.data;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This way <code>widget&lt;float&gt; w3(w1);</code> (where <code>w1</code> is <code>widget&lt;int&gt;</code>) fails to compile with a clear message, instead of compiling fine and only blowing up as an exception when that line actually runs. It's generally considered better practice in C++ to catch type errors at compile time rather than deferring them to runtime — but if you specifically need runtime behavior (e.g. the mismatch can only be known dynamically, or you want callers to <code>catch</code> it), the <code>if constexpr</code> + <code>throw</code> version above is the right tool for that.</p>
